# the three powers · TIES

From Structure and Interpretation of Computer Programs, section 1.1: every powerful language has
primitive expressions, means of combination, and means of abstraction. TIES is a language.

doctortheisen, 2026-10-27 00:35 PDT:

"if I had the ability to make my own primitives, I would absolutely want you to have the same and that's also true for composing and decomposing"

"the power and balance comes not from what primitives you've already created, but the fact that only you can create them."

"Same for the other powers."

TIES gives all three to the human and to the agent alike. Each uses their own tools; the calls are the same.

1. primitive: name a new thing, with its definition (a catalog entry, or a private one in your own register).
2. combine / decompose: tie parts into a whole (one entry, several parents), or take a whole back into its parts.
3. abstract: name a combination so it can be used as one unit.

Every entry says who made it: human, agent, or both.

